      <!-- H2 1: Complete Privacy -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Complete Privacy: A 100% Client-Side Private Text Deduplicator
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Modern list hygiene frequently involves highly sensitive datasets—including customer email newsletter rosters, unreleased product SKUs, internal server URLs, and proprietary database records. Typical online text utilities quietly transmit your clipboard data over HTTP POST requests to remote cloud instances for processing, introducing compliance violations under GDPR, CCPA, and enterprise confidentiality policies.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          FastToolVerse operates on a zero-compromise architectural standard: <strong>100% of text processing executes exclusively in your browser's local sandbox memory</strong>. No bytes are ever transmitted over the network, no cookies track your data, and no server logs are created. You can verify this by inspecting the browser Network tab or by disconnecting your internet connection entirely after loading the page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- H2 2: Algorithmic Architecture -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          High-Performance O(N) Hash Set Architecture vs Quadratic Filtering
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Naive text deduplication implementations rely on nested loops or <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">Array.prototype.indexOf()</code> / <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">Array.prototype.filter()</code>. In algorithmic terms, scanning an entire array for every single element introduces quadratic time complexity—<strong>O(N&sup2;)</strong>. When processing a dataset of 50,000 lines, a quadratic algorithm requires up to 2.5 billion comparison operations, inevitably freezing the browser thread and displaying a "Page Unresponsive" dialog.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          FastToolVerse utilizes modern ECMAScript <strong>Hash Sets (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">new Set()</code> / <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">new Map()</code>)</strong>. Hash sets provide near-instantaneous <strong>O(1)</strong> lookup and insertion complexity:
        </p>
        
        <div class="not-prose my-4 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 font-mono text-xs text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto space-y-2">
          <div><span class="text-slate-500">// High-performance linear O(N) deduplication and frequency tracking</span></div>
          <div>const keyMap = new Map();</div>
          <div>const uniqueLines = [];</div>
          <div class="pt-1">for (let i = 0; i &lt; lines.length; i++) &#123;</div>
          <div class="pl-4">let line = trimWhitespace ? lines[i].trim() : lines[i];</div>
          <div class="pl-4">if (removeEmpty &amp;&amp; !line) continue;</div>
          <div class="pl-4">let key = caseSensitive ? line : line.toLowerCase();</div>
          <div class="pl-4">if (ignorePunctuation) key = key.replace(/[^\p&#123;L&#125;\p&#123;N&#125;\s]/gu, '');</div>
          <div class="pl-4">if (!keyMap.has(key)) &#123;</div>
          <div class="pl-8">keyMap.set(key, &#123; original: line, count: 1 &#125;);</div>
          <div class="pl-4">&#125; else &#123;</div>
          <div class="pl-8">keyMap.get(key).count++;</div>
          <div class="pl-4">&#125;</div>
          <div>&#125;</div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          This linear <strong>O(N)</strong> architecture processes datasets containing 50,000+ entries in under 40 milliseconds, ensuring smooth, instantaneous responsiveness as you type, toggle checkboxes, or paste massive datasets.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- H2 3: How Whitespace and Case Sensitivity Affect Deduplication -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          How Whitespace Normalization &amp; Case Sensitivity Prevent False Positives
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          In real-world data, duplicates rarely appear with byte-for-byte identical formatting. Four common hidden variables prevent naive deduplicators from catching duplicates:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
          <li><strong>Hidden Whitespace &amp; Trailing Tabs:</strong> Copying rows from spreadsheets often attaches an invisible trailing tab (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">\t</code>) or trailing space to some rows. Enabling <em>Trim edge whitespace</em> normalizes each line before hashing, ensuring phantom spaces don't bypass duplicate detection.</li>
          <li><strong>Letter Casing Discrepancies:</strong> In CRM lists, email addresses often enter with mixed capitalization (e.g., <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">User@Domain.com</code> vs <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">user@domain.com</code>). In case-insensitive mode, the internal comparison key is lowered, eliminating duplicate messages while preserving the clean capitalization of the first appearance.</li>
          <li><strong>Punctuation &amp; Delimiter Variance:</strong> Entries with trailing commas, semicolons, or periods (such as items exported from mismatched databases) can be unified with the <em>Ignore punctuation & symbols</em> toggle.</li>
          <li><strong>Repetitive Blank Rows:</strong> Disjointed documents often separate sections with erratic sequences of blank lines. When <em>Remove empty/blank lines</em> is checked, all empty and whitespace-only lines are purged completely; when unchecked, only the first blank line is kept while repeated blank lines are cleaned.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- H2 4: Real-World Use Cases -->
